-- String interpolation of a String-typed operand must not cost a refcount
-- pair.  `"a${s}b"` desugars to `to_string(s)`, which Show-dispatches to
-- `Show$String.show` — a synthesized IDENTITY (a string already is its own
-- representation).  Lowering elides that call outright: emitting it and
-- relying on a later pass to inline it away is not free, because Perceus runs
-- FIRST and brackets the (still-present) owned-argument call with an
-- inc_rc/dec_rc pair that the subsequent inlining leaves stranded.
--
-- The gate is the perceus snapshot: `interp` must contain NO inc_rc on `s`,
-- and must lower to exactly the same shape as the hand-written `chain` below,
-- which passes the variable to string_concat3 directly.
mod InterpStringOperandRc do
  needs IO.Console
  fn interp(s : String) : String do
    "a${s}b"
  end

  fn chain(s : String) : String do
    "a" ++ s ++ "b"
  end

  fn main() do
    println(interp("x"))
    println(chain("x"))
  end
end
